 no data plane. The per-project enable/disable control acts on a configuration surface that the serving-side implementation defines and honors, so the Dashboard work builds against that surface. |\n| E001, E002, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes the capability that was built — schema, emission, and administration control must all be complete before docs are authored and technically reviewed. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 (schema contract from E001) | Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status (not payloads) |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | At-least-once delivery to the cluster audit sink; no loss on component restart |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | Administrators can enable/disable inference auditing per project from the Dashboard |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E001 | Common audit event schema defined and published for future components |\n| HLR-5 | P2 | E004 | Documentation covers enabling auditing and interpreting events |\n\nAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Epic Derivation Notes\n\n- **Priority inheritance (E001):** maps only to HLR-4 (P2), but it blocks all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2 events must conform to the schema), so it inherits P0.\n- **docs-authoring priority exception (E004):** maps to HLR-5 (P2) but derives P0 from the strategy frontmatter priority (Critical → P0). It does not push its priority upstream to the implementations it depends on.\n- **No Investigation epics:** the strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and states the audit sink interface is the standard cluster logging forwarder. No conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or risks tables exist. The remaining uncertainties (exact schema format, restart-survival buffering mechanism, per-project configuration surface) are implementation details the teams resolve when starting the work — captured as acceptance criteria, not flags (Step 6.5 two-way distinction).\n- **No onboarding chain:** both KServe and Dashboard exist in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and have architecture context files (in platform, Konflux onboarded). No new component provisioning is required.\n- **Component mapping:** E004 uses the canonical \"Documentation\" component from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. It has no architecture context file, but it is a docs work-product routing label, not a new platform code component — Rule 20's onboarding chain does not apply to `docs-authoring` epics.\n- **Teams (inferred):** the strategy provides no Impacted Teams table. Owners are inferred from architecture context: KServe → RHOAI Serving; Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard. E004 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as the primary capability owner — confirm ownership with the documentation team.\n- **Scope discipline:** the strategy describes the capability at a functional level; epic scope stays at that level. The schema format, buffering mechanism, configuration surface, and UI placement are left to the implementing teams.\n\n## Health Warnings\n\nNon-blocking — decomposition proceeds; human verification recommended:\n\n1. **Missing strategy sections:** no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements. The Affected Components section is a bare bullet list without change descriptions or owners. Decomposition proceeded with owners and interfaces inferred from architecture context; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n2. **Scope trap — \"tamper-evident\":** the problem statement demands \"tamper-evident, structured audit events,\" but no HLR captures tamper-evidence (e.g., integrity protection of the event stream); the HLRs only require structured events with at-least-once delivery. If auditors require cryptographic tamper-evidence, that is additional scope not covered by any HLR — flag to the strategy author. It would extend the emission epic's scope, not change epic structure.\n3. **Docs epic priority derivation:** E004 is P0 by the docs-authoring priority exception (strategy priority Critical) even though HLR-5 is P2. This is by design; flagged for reviewer awareness.\n4. **Unaddressed NFR:** HLR-2's \"not lost on component restart\" implies durable local buffering in the serving path; the latency/performance impact on inference traffic is not addressed by any NFR (none exist). Captured as an acceptance criterion on E002.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing. The audit sink is the standard cluster logging forwarder (in-platform), and no upstream contributions or third-party coordination are required.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":26770,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f72290dc62650fb","agent_id":"a0011decde189f5b8","session_id":"c994db2a-5c33-4a0a-873a-65ca036d690c","uuid":"c4fa2747-e740-4c96-8994-9e02e49f8a28","timestamp":"2026-10-21T22:12:26.541Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9010"}
