{"type":"assistant","message":{"id":"chatcmpl-0eb3eb41-3312-4a0e-9463-9e8626e6134b","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"chatcmpl-tool-9a85d9cfbb8ff624","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/private/var/folders/h5/l8qslbp55w5621qf9ngsh5jc0000gn/T/epic-RHAISTRAT-9010-_yr9p_f_/artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-9010-E002.md","content":"# Emit audit events for KServe inference traffic with at-least-once delivery\n\n## Description\n\nImplements audit event emission in the KServe serving path: every inference request through a KServe endpoint produces a structured audit event conforming to the common audit event schema, and events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics, surviving component restart. This epic also provides the per-project enable/disable configuration surface that the serving side honors and that the Dashboard administration control writes.\n\n## Scope\n\n- Instrument the KServe inference request path to emit one audit event per inference request (applicability across serverless and raw deployment modes is the implementing team's decision)\n- Audit event content per the common audit event schema: caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status — no request or response payloads\n- Deliver events to the standard cluster logging forwarder (cluster audit sink)\n- At-least-once delivery semantics with durable buffering so events are not lost on component restart (the buffering mechanism — e.g., spooling or queueing — is the implementing team's decision)\n- Per-project enable/disable configuration honored by the serving side, so inference auditing can be toggled per project\n- Unit and integration tests for emission, delivery, and restart survival\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event containing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status\n- Audit events conform to the common audit event schema\n- No request or response payloads appear in audit events\n- Events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics\n- Restarting the emitting component under traffic does not lose already-generated audit events\n- Inference auditing can be enabled and disabled per project via the serving-side configuration surface; disabled projects emit no inference audit events\n- The change passes the existing KServe build pipeline\n\n## HLR Traceability\n\n- HLR-1 (P0): Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, and response status (not payloads)\n- HLR-2 (P0): Audit events are delivered to the cluster audit sink with at-least-once semantics and are not lost on component restart\n- Supports HLR-3 (P1): provides the per-project configuration surface that the Dashboard administration control writes\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":33891,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-8f72290dc62650fb","agent_id":"a0011decde189f5b8","session_id":"c994db2a-5c33-4a0a-873a-65ca036d690c","uuid":"8a9ae66a-f263-4088-8428-735e46deb8c7","timestamp":"2026-10-21T22:12:55.065Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9010"}
