{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-aee0706e9f9b4411","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tepic_count: 4\n4\tcritical_path_length: 4\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\trevised: false\n7\ttriage: null\n8\ttriage_rationale: null\n9\t---\n10\t# Decomposition Summary — RHAISTRAT-9010: Audit logging for model inference traffic\n11\t\n12\t## Epic List\n13\t\n14\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n15\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n16\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E001 | Define and publish the common inference audit event schema | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n17\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E002 | Emit audit events for KServe inference traffic with at-least-once delivery | Implementation | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n18\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E003 | Dashboard administration control for per-project inference auditing | Implementation | RHOAI Dashboard | P1 |\n19\t| RHAISTRAT-9010-E004 | Document enabling inference auditing and interpreting audit events | Implementation (docs-authoring) | RHOAI Serving | P0 |\n20\t\n21\t## Dependency DAG\n22\t\n23\t```mermaid\n24\tgraph TD\n25\t    E001[\"E001: Audit event schema (P0)\"] --> E002[\"E002: KServe audit emission (P0)\"]\n26\t    E002 --> E003[\"E003: Dashboard per-project toggle (P1)\"]\n27\t    E001 --> E004[\"E004: Documentation (docs-authoring)\"]\n28\t    E002 --> E004\n29\t    E003 --> E004\n30\t```\n31\t\n32\tArrows point from dependency to dependent (E001 --> E002 means E001 must complete before E002 starts).\n33\t\n34\t## DAG Justification\n35\t\n36\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n37\t|------|------|-----------|\n38\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | HLR-4 explicitly states the schema is the format HLR-1's events must conform to and that HLR-1 cannot ship before the schema is settled. The emission implementation builds against the schema contract. |\n39\t| E002 → E003 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | Per Dashboard architecture context, the Dashboard reads and writes Kubernetes CRs and owns no data plane. The per-project enable/disable control acts on a configuration surface that the serving-side implementation defines and honors, so the Dashboard work builds against that surface. |\n40\t| E001, E002, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes the capability that was built — schema, emission, and administration control must all be complete before docs are authored and technically reviewed. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n41\t\n42\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n43\t\n44\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n45\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n46\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 (schema contract from E001) | Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status (not payloads) |\n47\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | At-least-once delivery to the cluster audit sink; no loss on component restart |\n48\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | Administrators can enable/disable inference auditing per project from the Dashboard |\n49\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E001 | Common audit event schema defined and published for future components |\n50\t| HLR-5 | P2 | E004 | Documentation covers enabling auditing and interpreting events |\n51\t\n52\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n53\t\n54\t## Epic Derivation Notes\n55\t\n56\t- **Priority inheritance (E001):** maps only to HLR-4 (P2), but it blocks all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2 events must conform to the schema), so it inherits P0.\n57\t- **docs-authoring priority exception (E004):** maps to HLR-5 (P2) but derives P0 from the strategy frontmatter priority (Critical → P0). It does not push its priority upstream to the implementations it depends on.\n58\t- **No Investigation epics:** the strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and states the audit sink interface is the standard cluster logging forwarder. No conditional