{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-95c3be0e48c8c658","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tstrat_id: RHAISTRAT-9010\n3\tscore: 13\n4\tpass: true\n5\trecommendation: accept\n6\tissues:\n7\t- severity: minor\n8\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n9\t  description: E001 ai_signals.human_process_gates=0 contradicts the E001 acceptance\n10\t    criterion (schema review completed with serving team and stakeholders) — a human\n11\t    review gate should score -1; inconsistent with E004 scoring -1 for its analogous\n12\t    technical-review AC\n13\t- severity: minor\n14\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n15\t  description: E001 ai_signals.existing_foundation=0 while its rationale describes\n16\t    the schema as a greenfield artifact — borderline per the rubric (greenfield maps\n17\t    to -1)\n18\t- severity: minor\n19\t  criterion: AI Implementability Scoring\n20\t  description: E001 ai_signals.architecture_claims=0 while its rationale admits the\n21\t    standard cluster logging forwarder audit-sink claim is unsubstantiated by architecture\n22\t    context — leans -1\n23\terror: null\n24\t---\n25\t# Decomposition Review — RHAISTRAT-9010: Audit logging for model inference traffic\n26\t\n27\t## Review Summary\n28\t\n29\tScore: 13/14 — pass\n30\tRecommendation: accept\n31\t\n32\tTriage check: `triage: null` (full decomposition) — 4 epics is expected, no triage contradiction.\n33\t\n34\t## Criterion Details\n35\t\n36\t### 1. HLR Coverage (2/2)\n37\t\n38\tAll 5 HLRs map to epics via the traceability matrix (HLR-1/HLR-2 → E002, HLR-3 → E003, HLR-4 → E001, HLR-5 → E004). Each epic's HLR Traceability section matches the matrix.\n39\t\n40\tPriority inheritance is correct: E001 maps only to HLR-4 (P2) but blocks all P0 work (HLR-4's note states HLR-1 events must conform to the schema and HLR-1 cannot ship first), so it correctly inherits P0. E004 is `docs-authoring` and is exempt from priority inheritance — its P0 derives from the strategy's Critical priority per the documented exception; its dependency on P1 E003 is not a violation. No priority collapse: no epic bundles deferrable lower-priority features with P0 work (E001's P2 HLR is already isolated in its own epic).\n41\t\n42\t### 2. DAG Coherence (2/2)\n43\t\n44\tDAG edges: E001→E002, E002→E003, E001→E004, E002→E004, E003→E004. Every diagram edge has a matching `dependencies` entry in the target epic's frontmatter and vice versa (E001: [], E002: [E001], E003: [E002], E004: [E001, E002, E003]). Arrows point dependency → dependent as the summary convention states. No cycles, no invalid edges.\n45\t\n46\tEdge justification verified against the rules:\n47\t- E001→E002 (Rule 13): the schema is a build-against contract (API), explicitly mandated by HLR-4's note — not a configuration reference.\n48\t- E002→E003 (Rule 13): E002 produces the per-project configuration surface (a CR/config API) that E003's Dashboard code writes; Dashboard architecture context confirms the Dashboard acts through Kubernetes CRs and owns no data plane. This is a genuine build-against artifact, not an image-digest/endpoint-URL configuration reference, so a `dependencies` edge (not just an AC gate) is correct.\n49\t- E001/E002/E003→E004 (Rule 11): docs-authoring blocked by all implementations — mandated, and correctly noted as not triggering priority inheritance.\n50\t\n51\tNo konflux-onboarding or license-validation epics exist, so rules 7/8/10 parallelism constraints are N/A. No conditional branch files exist, so branch frontmatter rules are N/A. Critical path length 4 (E001→E002→E003→E004) is within the M-standard heuristic (3-4) for a 2-component, 5-HLR strategy. Cross-check of epic content found no data/artifact dependency missing from the DAG.\n52\t\n53\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n54\t\n55\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: E001/E002 (KServe / RHOAI Serving), E003 (Dashboard / RHOAI Dashboard), E004 (Documentation / RHOAI Serving, doc