ntext, the Dashboard reads and writes Kubernetes CRs and owns no data plane. The per-project enable/disable control acts on a configuration surface that the serving-side implementation defines and honors, so the Dashboard work builds against that surface. |\n40\t| E001, E002, E003 → E004 | Rule 11 (docs-authoring always last) | Documentation describes the capability that was built — schema, emission, and administration control must all be complete before docs are authored and technically reviewed. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring priority exception). |\n41\t\n42\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n43\t\n44\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Coverage |\n45\t|-----|----------|---------|----------|\n46\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E002 (schema contract from E001) | Every inference request through a KServe endpoint emits an audit event capturing caller identity, model/version, timestamp, response status (not payloads) |\n47\t| HLR-2 | P0 | E002 | At-least-once delivery to the cluster audit sink; no loss on component restart |\n48\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E003 | Administrators can enable/disable inference auditing per project from the Dashboard |\n49\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E001 | Common audit event schema defined and published for future components |\n50\t| HLR-5 | P2 | E004 | Documentation covers enabling auditing and interpreting events |\n51\t\n52\tAll HLRs covered; no orphaned requirements.\n53\t\n54\t## Epic Derivation Notes\n55\t\n56\t- **Priority inheritance (E001):** maps only to HLR-4 (P2), but it blocks all P0 work (HLR-1/HLR-2 events must conform to the schema), so it inherits P0.\n57\t- **docs-authoring priority exception (E004):** maps to HLR-5 (P2) but derives P0 from the strategy frontmatter priority (Critical → P0). It does not push its priority upstream to the implementations it depends on.\n58\t- **No Investigation epics:** the strategy declares \"Open Questions: None\" and states the audit sink interface is the standard cluster logging forwarder. No conditional ADRs, pending reviews, or risks tables exist. The remaining uncertainties (exact schema format, restart-survival buffering mechanism, per-project configuration surface) are implementation details the teams resolve when starting the work — captured as acceptance criteria, not flags (Step 6.5 two-way distinction).\n59\t- **No onboarding chain:** both KServe and Dashboard exist in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and have architecture context files (in platform, Konflux onboarded). No new component provisioning is required.\n60\t- **Component mapping:** E004 uses the canonical \"Documentation\" component from `.context/rhai-components.txt`. It has no architecture context file, but it is a docs work-product routing label, not a new platform code component — Rule 20's onboarding chain does not apply to `docs-authoring` epics.\n61\t- **Teams (inferred):** the strategy provides no Impacted Teams table. Owners are inferred from architecture context: KServe → RHOAI Serving; Dashboard → RHOAI Dashboard. E004 is assigned to RHOAI Serving as the primary capability owner — confirm ownership with the documentation team.\n62\t- **Scope discipline:** the strategy describes the capability at a functional level; epic scope stays at that level. The schema format, buffering mechanism, configuration surface, and UI placement are left to the implementing teams.\n63\t\n64\t## Health Warnings\n65\t\n66\tNon-blocking — decomposition proceeds; human verification recommended:\n67\t\n68\t1. **Missing strategy sections:** no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then Acceptance Criteria, no Non-Functional Requirements. The Affected Components section is a bare bullet list without change descriptions or owners. Decomposition proceeded with owners and interfaces inferred from architecture context; epic acceptance criteria were derived from the HLRs.\n69\t2. **Scope trap — \"tamper-evident\":** the problem statement demands \"tamper-evident, structured audit events,\" but no HLR